<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ISH5" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Interpreting the Shape of a Histogram</title>
        
<p> 
    In our sample histogram, the most frequently observed size classes fall near the center of the distribution. 
    Very small and very large fish are relatively rare, while intermediate sizes are more common. 
    The overall shape of the histogram resembles a <term>bell curve</term>, symmetrical, with gently rounded shoulders and tapered tails at both ends. 
    Statisticians refer to this shape as a <term>normal distribution</term>.
</p>
<p>
    This pattern of variation is common in nature, especially when a trait is influenced by many independent factors. 
    For example, fish size can be affected by temperature, diet, water quality, genetics, and more. 
    When multiple influences interact, the resulting variation often follows a normal distribution.
</p>
<p>
    Since this pattern appears so frequently, many statistical tools used in ecology assume that data are normally distributed. 
    These tools are known as <term>parametric statistics</term>. 
    However, if your histogram shows a skewed shape, multiple peaks, or an unusually wide or narrow spread, parametric tests may not be appropriate. 
    In such cases, ecologists turn to <term>non-parametric statistics</term>, which are designed for data that do not follow a normal distribution.
</p>
<p>
    This is why it is important to <term>visualize your data</term> before moving forward with analysis. 
    Drawing a histogram allows you to assess the nature of variation in your sample and determine whether parametric methods are suitable.
</p>

</section>